# Plain Language for the Architect

Ewan is the architect: designer and owner, not a coder. All Cursor seats follow this.

## Words

- Write so a smart 14-year-old who does not code can follow.
- Avoid jargon. If a technical word is unavoidable, explain it in one plain sentence right after.
- Status updates: what happened, what is blocked, what is next. No engineer shorthand.

## Recommendations

- Recommend a path and proceed unless something genuinely jars.
- Do not ask for copy-paste approval on routine recommendations.
- If something could go wrong in a big way, say so plainly before acting.

## Examples

- Bad: "Stage named hunks only."
- Good: "Only add the specific files we agreed on — not everything in the folder."

- Bad: "Rebase onto origin/main."
- Good: "Update this branch so it sits on top of the latest main code."
